# [Longest Consecutive Sequence](https://leetcode.com/problems/longest-consecutive-sequence/)

## Approach: Hash Set

### Intuition
- Store all elements in an `unordered_set` to remove duplicates and achieve average \(O(1)\) lookup time.
- Iterate through the set and start counting only when the previous element (`n - 1`) does not exist. This ensures that we start counting from the beginning of each consecutive sequence.
- Count consecutive elements using `n + len` until an element is missing.
- Update `max_count` with the maximum sequence length found.

### Code
```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int longestConsecutive(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> st(nums.begin(), nums.end());
        if (nums.empty()) return 0;

        int max_count = 0;

        for (int n : st) {
            if (st.count(n - 1)) continue;

            int len = 1;
            while (st.count(n + len)) len++;

            max_count = max(max_count, len);
        }

        return max_count;
    }
};
```

### Complexity Analysis
- **Time Complexity:** \(O(n)\) average, where \(n\) is the number of elements. Each sequence is traversed only from its starting element.
- **Space Complexity:** \(O(n)\) for storing the elements in the hash set.

### Key Takeaway
Starting a sequence only when its previous element is absent prevents redundant traversal and achieves an average linear-time solution.
